{"graph_chars": "Demand is the amount of a good or service consumers are willing and able to purchase at each price. Willing and able. A person with no driver's license has no effective demand for a car. Price is what a buyer pays for one unit. Quantity demanded is how many units consumers would buy at that price. A rise in price almost always cuts quantity demanded. A fall in price raises it. That inverse relationship, holding other things constant, is the law of demand. A demand schedule is the table. A demand curve is the graph: quantity on the horizontal axis, price on the vertical. For gasoline: at one dollar a gallon, eight hundred million gallons. At two dollars twenty, four hundred twenty million. Nearly all demand curves slope down from left to right. Exam trap: demand is the whole curve. Quantity demanded is one point on it. A change in the good's own price moves you along the curve. It does not shift demand.", "graph_times": [[0.0, 0.0665], [0.0665, 0.133], [0.133, 0.1995], [0.1995, 0.2661], [0.2661, 0.3326], [0.3326, 0.3991], [0.3991, 0.4656], [0.4656, 0.5321], [0.5321, 0.5986], [0.5986, 0.6651], [0.6651, 0.7317], [0.7317, 0.7982], [0.7982, 0.8647], [0.8647, 0.9312], [0.9312, 0.9977], [0.9977, 1.0642], [1.0642, 1.1307], [1.1307, 1.1972], [1.1972, 1.2638], [1.2638, 1.3303], [1.3303, 1.3968], [1.3968, 1.4633], [1.4633, 1.5298], [1.5298, 1.5963], [1.5963, 1.6628], [1.6628, 1.7294], [1.7294, 1.7959], [1.7959, 1.8624], [1.8624, 1.9289], [1.9289, 1.9954], [1.9954, 2.0619], [2.0619, 2.1284], [2.1284, 2.195], [2.195, 2.2615], [2.2615, 2.328], [2.328, 2.3945], [2.3945, 2.461], [2.461, 2.5275], [2.5275, 2.594], [2.594, 2.6605], [2.6605, 2.7271], [2.7271, 2.7936], [2.7936, 2.8601], [2.8601, 2.9266], [2.9266, 2.9931], [2.9931, 3.0596], [3.0596, 3.1261], [3.1261, 3.1927], [3.1927, 3.2592], [3.2592, 3.3257], [3.3257, 3.3922], [3.3922, 3.4587], [3.4587, 3.5252], [3.5252, 3.5917], [3.5917, 3.6583], [3.6583, 3.7248], [3.7248, 3.7913], [3.7913, 3.8578], [3.8578, 3.9243], 